From 73f45689986f79fe88b1b01f3613f8a79a53709a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: magnus_python <miguelsensacion@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 11:33:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?7z:=20AES256GCM=20coder=20=E2=80=94=20AES-256-G?=
 =?UTF-8?q?CM=20+=20Argon2id=20method=20(ID=2006=20F7=2011=2001)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Septima encryption expansion, patch 3/N: the coder and full 7z-format
wiring. Selected with -mem=AES256GCM (default remains 7zAES); works
with -mhe=on so encrypted headers use the same method.

- Crypto/7zGcm.{h,cpp} + 7zGcmRegister.cpp: ICompressCoder pairing
  Argon2id (RFC 9106, key from UTF-16LE password bytes + 16-byte random
  salt) with AES-256-GCM (96-bit random IV per folder, pack stream =
  ciphertext || 16-byte tag, constant-time tag compare on decode; tag
  mismatch => S_FALSE i.e. wrong password / corrupted data).
  Key cache mirrors 7zAes (global + per-coder, CS-locked) — load-
  bearing, since each Argon2id derivation costs ~0.2 s / 256 MiB.
  Defaults m=256 MiB t=3 p=4; decoder enforces ceilings (m <= 2 GiB,
  t <= 64, p <= 64) so hostile headers cannot demand absurd resources.
- 7zHeader.h: k_AES256GCM = 0x6F71101 (F7 11 xx vendor range under the
  06 crypto type byte); documented in DOC/Methods-Extern.md with the
  full coder-props layout.
- 7zCompressionMode.h/7zEncode.cpp: CryptoMethod field replaces the
  hardcoded k_AES at both insertion sites (content + header folders).
- 7zHandlerOut.cpp: -mem= parsing for 7z (aes256|aes256cbc|aes256gcm).
- 7zHandler.cpp/7zItem.h: encrypted-flag detection + method display
  ("AES256GCM:256m:t3:p4" in l -slt).
- makefiles: AesGcm.o, argon2 objects (portable ref.c fill path for
  now; -Wno-type-limits for vendored argon2 under -Werror).

Tested (Linux x64): create/list/extract round-trip (LZMA2+AES256GCM);
wrong password rejected via tag; 1-byte ciphertext tamper rejected;
-mhe=on list-without-password refused, extract OK; default archives
still legacy 7zAES:19; stock 7zz 26.02 lists the archive and fails
extraction gracefully ("Unsupported Method", no crash).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
